  "image_name": "4e58537d44f68df3d69a8bf5a025f6a7.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how lipoproteins and inflammation contribute to atherosclerosis in the heart’s arteries. It centers on the balance between LDL, HDL, and related particles in the bloodstream and shows how this balance influences cholesterol buildup in the vessel wall.\n\nLDL, labeled with ApoB, appears to deposit cholesterol in the artery and promote plaque growth. As the process advances, LDL is oxidized, macrophages are recruited, and inflammatory markers such as IL-6 and CRP are shown near the developing lesion. Another particle, Lp(a), carrying oxidized phospholipids, is also depicted as contributing to this inflammatory, pro-atherogenic environment. Over time, these events lead to visible cholesterol accumulation inside the arterial wall.\n\nIn contrast, HDL and ApoA-I are shown in a protective pathway, participating in phospholipid exchange and reverse cholesterol transport back to the liver. CETP-mediated exchange between LDL and HDL is also indicated, suggesting lipid transfer between these particles.\n\nThe overall message is a comparison between harmful lipid-driven plaque progression and protective cholesterol removal. As plaque enlarges, the figure emphasizes the clinical consequence: potential plaque rupture, thrombus formation, and an acute cardiovascular event.",
